# ============ CHAPTER 3 ============
h1("Chapter 3: Requirement of Proposed System")
body('This chapter provides a comprehensive definition of the requirements and constraints governing the proposed system, Dione. A thorough requirement analysis is essential for ensuring that the final system meets the expectations of its users and operates reliably within its intended environment. The chapter outlines the functional requirements that define what the system must do, the non-functional requirements that define how well the system must perform, the constraints that limit the design space, the hardware and software specifications, the techniques and tools employed during development, and the alignment of the project with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals.')

h2("3.1 Functional Requirements")
body('The functional requirements describe the core operations and capabilities that the Dione system must provide to its users. These requirements were derived from the problem definition, the analysis of existing system limitations, and the feedback received from domain experts during the literature survey phase. Each functional requirement has been carefully specified to ensure completeness, consistency, and testability.')
body('The system shall accept user input in natural language through multiple interface modalities, including a Command-Line Interface (CLI) for power users who prefer text-based interaction, and a web-based graphical user interface for users who prefer a visual interaction paradigm. The natural language processing capability must support a wide range of input styles, from simple commands like "send an email to John" to complex, multi-part instructions like "find all PDF files in my downloads folder that were modified this week, summarize each one, and email the summaries to my team."')
body('The system shall process all user input using a locally deployed Large Language Model (LLM), ensuring that no user data is transmitted to external servers during the reasoning phase. The LLM must be capable of understanding user intent, extracting relevant parameters from natural language instructions, and generating structured action plans that specify the sequence of operations needed to fulfill the user\'s request. The system must support multiple LLM backends, including models served through Ollama and Llama.cpp, to provide flexibility in model selection based on available hardware resources and task requirements.')
body('The system shall convert unstructured LLM outputs into structured, executable commands using well-defined JSON schemas. Each command must specify the target plugin, the function to be called, and all required parameters in a format that can be validated against a predefined schema before execution. This structured approach ensures that the system can reliably parse and execute commands while maintaining a clear audit trail of all operations performed.')
body('The system shall identify and select appropriate tools and plugins for execution based on the nature of the user\'s request. The plugin selection process must consider factors such as the type of action required, the availability of the requested plugin, and the permissions associated with the operation. The system shall execute a comprehensive range of tasks including file retrieval and manipulation on the local file system, composing and sending emails through the Gmail API, reading and responding to messages on messaging platforms, managing calendar events, executing system commands, and interacting with web browsers.')
body('The system shall support multi-step task execution using the ReAct iterative loop, where the system reasons about the current state, performs an action, observes the result, and decides on the next step. This capability is essential for handling complex workflows that require sequential operations with conditional logic, such as "if the latest email from the client contains an attachment, download it, summarize its contents, and draft a response." The system shall provide feedback and final output to the user after each operation, maintaining transparency about what actions were taken and their results.')
body('The system shall maintain contextual memory using a vector database powered by ChromaDB, enabling it to recall information from previous interactions, user preferences, and accumulated knowledge. This memory system must support efficient retrieval of relevant context based on semantic similarity, ensuring that the system can provide personalized and contextually appropriate responses. The system shall validate all actions before execution to ensure correctness, safety, and compliance with user-defined permission policies.')

h2("3.2 Non-Functional Requirements")
body('The non-functional requirements define the quality attributes and operational characteristics that the Dione system must exhibit. These requirements are critical for ensuring that the system provides a satisfactory user experience and operates reliably in real-world conditions.')
body('In terms of performance, the system should provide low latency with a Time-to-Action (TTA) of less than three seconds for simple, single-step tasks. For complex multi-step workflows, the system should begin executing the first action within five seconds of receiving the user\'s instruction. The system should maintain responsive interaction even during long-running operations by providing progress updates and intermediate results to the user.')
body('For security, all actions must be validated against predefined safety policies before execution. The validation process must check for potentially dangerous operations such as file deletion, system modification, and unauthorized network access. High-risk operations must require explicit user confirmation before proceeding. The system must implement sandboxed execution environments for plugins to prevent malicious or erroneous plugin code from affecting the core system or the user\'s operating system.')
body('Regarding privacy, no sensitive user data should be transmitted outside the local system under any circumstances. All LLM inference, data processing, and action execution must occur entirely on the user\'s device. The system should not collect telemetry data, usage statistics, or any other information that could be used to identify or profile the user. Stored data, including conversation history and user preferences, should be encrypted at rest to protect against unauthorized physical access to the device.')
body('The system should handle errors gracefully without crashing or losing state, ensuring reliability. When an action fails, the system should provide a clear error message, suggest potential remedies, and offer to retry the operation with modified parameters. The system should maintain its operational state across restarts, preserving conversation history, user preferences, and plugin configurations.')
body('New plugins and functionalities should be easily integrable without requiring modifications to the core system, supporting scalability. The plugin interface should be well-documented and straightforward, enabling developers to create new plugins with minimal effort. The system should support hot-loading of plugins, allowing new capabilities to be added without restarting the system.')
body('The interface should be simple, intuitive, and user-friendly, promoting usability. Users should be able to interact with the system using natural language without needing to learn special commands or syntax. The system should provide helpful suggestions and auto-completion features to guide users in formulating their requests. Error messages and system feedback should be expressed in plain language that is accessible to non-technical users.')
body('The code structure should follow clean architecture principles and support easy updates and modifications for long-term maintainability. Each module should have a single, well-defined responsibility, and dependencies between modules should be minimized. The codebase should include comprehensive documentation, including inline comments, API documentation, and architectural decision records.')

h2("3.3 Constraints")
body('The system operates under several constraints that limit the design space and impose trade-offs between competing objectives. Understanding these constraints is essential for making informed architectural decisions and setting realistic expectations for system performance.')
body('Hardware limitations represent the most significant constraint on the system\'s capabilities. The performance of locally deployed LLMs is directly dependent on the available computational resources, particularly RAM and GPU capabilities. Larger, more capable models require more memory and processing power, which may not be available on all target devices. For systems with limited GPU capabilities, inference times may be significantly longer, potentially degrading the user experience. The system must be designed to operate effectively across a range of hardware configurations, from high-end workstations with dedicated GPUs to mid-range laptops with integrated graphics.')
body('Model size constraints necessitate the use of quantization techniques to reduce the memory footprint and computational requirements of LLMs for efficient execution on consumer hardware. While quantization enables the deployment of larger models on resource-constrained devices, it may introduce some degradation in model quality, particularly for complex reasoning tasks. The system must carefully balance model size, inference speed, and output quality to provide the best possible experience within the available hardware constraints.')
body('Processing power limitations mean that CPU-only systems may experience significantly slower inference compared to systems with dedicated GPUs. The system should detect the available hardware at startup and configure its inference pipeline accordingly, selecting appropriate model sizes and quantization levels based on the detected capabilities. Security restrictions through strict validation may limit certain automated actions, particularly those that involve sensitive operations such as file deletion, system configuration changes, or sending messages. While these restrictions are necessary for safety, they may occasionally prevent legitimate actions, requiring the user to provide explicit confirmation.')
body('Integration constraints arise from the fact that external APIs and services may have rate limits, authentication requirements, or usage policies that restrict the system\'s ability to interact with them. The system must handle these constraints gracefully, queuing requests when rate limits are reached and providing clear feedback to the user when external service limitations prevent the completion of a requested task. Offline limitations mean that some functionalities requiring internet access, such as sending emails, checking web content, or accessing cloud-based APIs, will not be available when the device lacks network connectivity. The system should clearly communicate these limitations to the user and suggest alternative actions that can be performed offline.')

h2("3.4 Hardware and Software Requirements")
h3("3.4.1 Hardware Requirements")
body('The hardware requirements for the Dione system have been specified to support the computational demands of local LLM inference, vector database operations, and concurrent plugin execution. The minimum hardware configuration includes an Intel Core i5 or AMD Ryzen 5 processor or higher to provide adequate processing power for model inference and system operations. A minimum of 16 GB of system RAM is required, with 32 GB recommended for optimal performance when running larger language models. An NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU with at least 8 GB of VRAM, or Apple Silicon with unified memory architecture, is recommended for accelerated model inference. A minimum of 50 GB of SSD storage is required for storing the operating system, language models, vector database, and user data, with SSD storage strongly preferred over traditional HDD for improved I/O performance during model loading and data retrieval operations.')

h3("3.4.2 Software Requirements")
body('The software requirements encompass the programming languages, frameworks, libraries, and tools necessary for developing and deploying the Dione system. Python 3.10 or higher serves as the primary programming language, chosen for its extensive ecosystem of AI and machine learning libraries, its readability, and its cross-platform compatibility. Ollama and Llama.cpp serve as the LLM backend frameworks, providing efficient local inference capabilities with support for a wide range of open-source language models including LLaMA 2, Mistral 7B, and their derivatives. ChromaDB serves as the vector database for storing and retrieving document embeddings, conversation history, and contextual information, while SQLite provides lightweight relational database capabilities for logging, configuration management, and structured data storage. Key libraries include PyTorch for tensor operations and model management, Pydantic for data validation and schema enforcement, and FastAPI for building the backend API server. External API integrations include the Gmail API for email operations, the Google Calendar API for scheduling, and OS-level scripting tools for system interactions.')

h2("3.5 Techniques Utilized for the Proposed System")
body('The development of Dione employs several advanced techniques drawn from the fields of artificial intelligence, software engineering, and information security. The ReAct (Reason + Act) Loop serves as the core operational paradigm, enabling the system to iteratively reason about a task, take action, observe the result, and refine its approach. This technique was directly inspired by the ReAct paper reviewed in the literature survey and has been adapted to work within Dione\'s local execution environment. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances the system\'s context awareness by retrieving relevant information from the vector database to augment the LLM\'s input. This technique enables the system to maintain long-term memory across sessions and provide personalized responses based on the user\'s interaction history.')
body('Prompt Engineering is employed to structure model inputs in a way that consistently generates actionable, well-formatted outputs. Carefully designed system prompts define the model\'s role, specify the expected output format, and provide examples of correct tool usage. Model Quantization techniques, including GGUF and AWQ formats, are used to reduce model size and memory requirements for efficient local inference without significant loss of quality. Schema Validation using Pydantic models ensures the correctness and completeness of all generated commands before they are passed to the execution engine, catching malformed outputs and preventing potentially harmful operations from being executed.')

h2("3.6 Tools Utilized for the Proposed System")
body('The following tools and platforms are used in the implementation of Dione. Ollama provides a streamlined interface for downloading, managing, and running local LLMs, supporting model switching and configuration through a simple API. Llama.cpp offers highly optimized C++ inference code for running quantized models on CPU and GPU, providing the fastest possible inference speeds on consumer hardware. ChromaDB is an open-source vector database designed for AI applications, providing efficient storage and retrieval of high-dimensional embeddings with support for metadata filtering and semantic search. SQLite is used as a lightweight, serverless database for storing system logs, plugin configurations, and structured metadata. Python serves as the core development language, leveraging its extensive ecosystem of AI, web development, and system administration libraries. Visual Studio Code serves as the primary development environment, with extensions for Python development, debugging, and code quality analysis. Git and GitHub provide version control and collaborative development capabilities, enabling the team to manage code changes, track issues, and coordinate development efforts.')

h2("3.7 SDGs Mapped with the Proposed Project and Justification")
body('The Dione project contributes to several United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), demonstrating its alignment with global priorities for sustainable and equitable technological development.')
body('Under SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure), the project promotes the development of advanced AI infrastructure that can operate independently of centralized cloud services. By enabling sophisticated AI capabilities on consumer hardware, Dione contributes to the democratization of AI technology, making powerful tools accessible to individuals and organizations that may not have the resources to invest in cloud computing infrastructure. The project encourages innovation in local, privacy-first computing systems, advancing the frontier of edge AI and decentralized computing.')
body('Under SDG 8 (Decent Work and Economic Growth), the project automates repetitive digital tasks, improving individual and organizational productivity. By handling routine operations such as email management, file organization, and scheduling, Dione frees users to focus on higher-value creative and strategic work. The project also supports the development of intelligent tools for professional use, contributing to the growth of the AI tools industry and creating opportunities for developers to build and distribute specialized plugins.')
body('Under SDG 16 (Peace, Justice and Strong Institutions), the project ensures data privacy and security by eliminating the need to share sensitive information with external servers. This approach reduces the risks associated with centralized data storage and processing, including unauthorized access, data breaches, and surveillance. By giving users complete control over their data, Dione supports the principles of digital sovereignty and individual privacy rights that are increasingly recognized as fundamental human rights in the digital age.')
body('Under SDG 4 (Quality Education), the project provides a platform for learning advanced AI concepts including language model inference, retrieval-augmented generation, agent architectures, and plugin-based software design. The open-source nature of the project and its modular architecture make it an excellent educational resource for students and researchers interested in exploring the intersection of AI, systems programming, and information security. The project encourages research in agentic AI and system design, contributing to the advancement of knowledge in these rapidly evolving fields.')
