# ============ CHAPTER 4 ============
h1("Chapter 4: Proposed Design")
body('This chapter presents a comprehensive description of the design aspects of the proposed system, Dione. It includes detailed architectural representations such as block diagrams and modular diagrams, system design models including data flow diagrams, use case diagrams, flowcharts, activity diagrams, state transition diagrams, and entity-relationship diagrams. The chapter also discusses the algorithms used in existing systems and presents the project scheduling and tracking plan. The design philosophy emphasizes the integration of reasoning and execution capabilities while maintaining strict privacy controls, modular architecture for extensibility, and robust security mechanisms for safe autonomous operation.')

h2("4.1 Block Diagram Representation of the Proposed System")
ip("Figure 4.1 \u2013 Block Diagram of the Dione System")
body('The block diagram represents the high-level architecture and data flow within the Dione system, illustrating how user inputs are transformed into executed actions through a series of processing stages. The architecture follows a layered design pattern where each layer has a specific responsibility and communicates with adjacent layers through well-defined interfaces.')
body('The User Interface Layer serves as the entry point for all user interactions. It accepts natural language input through either the CLI or the web-based GUI and displays results, progress updates, and error messages back to the user. The interface layer is responsible for parsing raw user input, formatting it for consumption by the reasoning layer, and presenting system outputs in a human-readable format. The design supports multiple concurrent interface sessions, allowing users to interact with the system through different modalities simultaneously.')
body('The Context Manager is the second processing stage, responsible for enriching the user\'s raw input with relevant contextual information retrieved from the system\'s memory. Using RAG techniques, the Context Manager queries the ChromaDB vector database to find semantically similar past interactions, relevant documents, and stored user preferences. This contextual information is appended to the user\'s input before it is passed to the LLM, enabling the model to generate more accurate and personalized responses. The Context Manager also maintains the current conversation history, ensuring continuity across multiple turns of interaction.')
body('The Local LLM (Reasoning Engine) is the core intelligence of the system. It receives the context-augmented input and generates structured outputs in the form of JSON commands that specify the actions to be performed. The Reasoning Engine operates entirely on the local device, using frameworks such as Ollama or Llama.cpp for model inference. It implements the ReAct loop, alternating between reasoning steps (where the model analyzes the situation and plans actions) and action steps (where the model generates specific tool calls). The engine supports multiple model backends and can be configured to use different models for different types of tasks.')
body('The Validation Layer (Security Kernel) acts as a critical security checkpoint between the reasoning and execution stages. Every command generated by the LLM is validated against predefined schemas using Pydantic models to ensure structural correctness. The layer also checks commands against security policies, verifying that the requested operation is permitted, that all required parameters are present and valid, and that the command does not attempt any prohibited actions. High-risk operations, such as file deletion or system modification, trigger a confirmation request that requires explicit user approval before proceeding.')
body('The Plugin Registry identifies and invokes the appropriate tool for executing each validated command. It maintains a registry of all available plugins, their capabilities, and their interfaces. When a command is received, the registry matches it to the appropriate plugin based on the specified tool name and function. The registry supports dynamic plugin loading, allowing new plugins to be added to the system without restarting or modifying the core engine.')
body('The Execution Layer performs the actual actions specified by the validated commands. Each plugin operates in a sandboxed environment that isolates its execution from the core system and other plugins. The execution layer handles the communication between plugins and external systems such as email servers, messaging APIs, and the local file system. After each action is completed, the results are fed back into the Context Manager and the Reasoning Engine, enabling the ReAct loop to continue with the next step of the workflow.')

h2("4.2 Modular Diagram Representation of the Proposed System")
ip("Figure 4.2 \u2013 Modular Diagram of the Dione System")
body('The modular diagram provides a detailed view of the internal architecture of Dione, emphasizing the separation of concerns and the interfaces between individual modules. Each module is designed as an independent, self-contained unit with a clearly defined responsibility and a well-documented API, enabling independent development, testing, and deployment.')
body('The Context Manager Module handles all aspects of memory retrieval and management using ChromaDB as its underlying storage engine. It maintains conversation history across sessions, stores document embeddings for semantic search, and manages user preference profiles. The module implements efficient indexing and retrieval algorithms that enable sub-second context retrieval even with large memory stores. It also includes a memory pruning mechanism that automatically archives old or low-relevance memories to maintain performance as the memory store grows over time.')
body('The Model Adapter Module provides a unified interface for connecting to different LLM backends, including Ollama for easy model management and Llama.cpp for high-performance inference. The adapter handles all backend-specific communication protocols, model loading, and inference configuration, presenting a consistent API to the rest of the system regardless of which backend is in use. This abstraction layer ensures compatibility across different models and backends, allowing users to switch between models without any changes to the rest of the system.')
body('The Plugin Registry Module manages the lifecycle of all plugins in the system. It handles plugin discovery, loading, initialization, and shutdown. The registry automatically generates tool descriptions from plugin function signatures, creating the schema information that the LLM needs to correctly invoke each tool. It also manages plugin dependencies, ensuring that plugins are loaded in the correct order and that all required dependencies are available before a plugin is activated.')
body('The Safety Guardrail Module is responsible for detecting and preventing unsafe or unauthorized operations. It implements a multi-layered security model that includes input validation (checking user inputs for injection attacks), output validation (verifying that LLM-generated commands conform to expected schemas), action authorization (checking commands against permission policies), and execution monitoring (detecting and halting anomalous behavior during plugin execution). The module maintains a configurable set of security policies that can be customized by the user to match their risk tolerance and operational requirements.')
body('The Execution Engine is the final module in the processing pipeline, responsible for executing validated commands through the appropriate plugins and returning results to the system. It manages concurrent plugin executions, handles timeouts and retries, and provides detailed logging of all executed actions for audit and debugging purposes. The engine implements a transactional execution model where multi-step operations can be rolled back if an intermediate step fails, ensuring system consistency even in the face of errors.')

h2("4.3 Design of the Proposed System")
ip("Figure 4.3 \u2013 Use Case Diagram")
ip("Figure 4.4 \u2013 Data Flow Diagram (Level 0)")
ip("Figure 4.5 \u2013 Data Flow Diagram (Level 1)")

h3("4.3.1 Data Flow Diagram (DFD)")
body('The Data Flow Diagram illustrates how data moves through the Dione system from input to output. At Level 0 (Context Level), the diagram shows the system as a single process that receives natural language input from the User, interacts with External Systems (email servers, messaging platforms, file system, calendar services), and returns Output (action results, confirmations, error messages) to the user. At Level 1, the data flow is decomposed into six major processes: Input Processing, which receives and normalizes user input; Context Enrichment, which retrieves relevant memories and augments the input; LLM Reasoning, which generates action plans based on the enriched input; Command Validation, which verifies the safety and correctness of generated commands; Plugin Execution, which carries out the validated commands through appropriate tools; and Output Formatting, which presents results to the user in a readable format. The data flows between these processes are clearly defined, with each flow carrying specific types of information such as raw text, structured commands, execution results, and error reports.')

h3("4.3.2 Use Case Diagram")
body('The Use Case Diagram identifies the actors that interact with the Dione system and the use cases that describe the system\'s behavior from the users\u2019 perspective. The primary actor is the User, who initiates all interactions by providing natural language instructions. The system actor, Dione, processes these instructions and autonomously executes the required actions. External Applications, including email clients, messaging platforms, calendars, and file systems, serve as secondary actors that are invoked by the system during task execution. The key use cases include providing natural language input, retrieving contextual information from memory, generating action plans through LLM reasoning, validating actions for safety and correctness, executing tasks through plugins, monitoring task progress, and receiving results and confirmations.')

h3("4.3.3 Flowchart")
ip("Figure 4.6 \u2013 System Flowchart (ReAct Loop)")
body('The system flowchart provides a detailed representation of the ReAct loop, which is the core operational mechanism of Dione. The flow begins when the system accepts user input and enriches it with contextual information. The LLM then generates a reasoning step (Thought) that analyzes the current situation and determines the appropriate next action. Based on this reasoning, the LLM generates an Action specification in JSON format. The action is validated by the Security Kernel, and if validation fails, the system generates an error message and prompts the LLM to revise its approach. If validation succeeds, the action is executed through the appropriate plugin. The execution result (Observation) is fed back to the LLM, which evaluates whether the overall task is complete. If more actions are needed, the loop continues with another Thought-Action-Observation cycle. When the task is complete, the system formats and presents the final results to the user.')

h3("4.3.4 Activity Diagram")
body('The Activity Diagram models the dynamic workflow of the Dione system, showing the sequence of activities and the decision points that govern the flow of execution. The initial activity is receiving user input, followed by context retrieval and input augmentation. The system then enters the ReAct loop, where a decision node evaluates whether the LLM has generated a final answer or an action command. If a final answer is generated, the system formats and displays it to the user. If an action command is generated, a second decision node checks whether the action passes validation. Valid actions proceed to plugin execution, while invalid actions trigger error handling and re-prompting of the LLM. After successful execution, the observation is recorded and the loop returns to the LLM for further reasoning. This activity diagram clearly shows the iterative nature of the system\'s operation and the multiple decision points that ensure safe and correct execution.')

h3("4.3.5 State Transition Diagram")
body('The State Transition Diagram models the different operational states of the Dione system and the events that trigger transitions between them. The system starts in the Idle state, waiting for user input. Upon receiving input, it transitions to the Processing state, where the input is normalized and context is retrieved. The system then moves to the Reasoning state, where the LLM generates thoughts and action plans. When an action is generated, the system transitions to the Validating state, where the Security Kernel checks the action\'s safety and correctness. If validation succeeds, the system enters the Executing state, where the plugin performs the requested operation. After execution, the system either returns to the Reasoning state for further actions or transitions to the Completed state, where results are presented to the user and the system returns to the Idle state. Error conditions can trigger transitions to an Error state from any operational state, with recovery mechanisms that attempt to return the system to a stable state.')

h3("4.3.6 Entity-Relationship (ER) Diagram")
body('The Entity-Relationship Diagram models the data entities within the Dione system and their relationships. The primary entities are User (representing the system operator with attributes such as preferences and permission settings), Task (representing a user request with attributes including input text, status, and timestamps), Plugin (representing an executable tool with attributes such as name, description, and capabilities), Execution Log (recording each action performed with attributes including command, result, and timestamp), and Context Memory (storing historical interactions and knowledge with attributes including embedding vector, content, and metadata). The relationships between these entities are: a User generates multiple Tasks, each Task utilizes one or more Plugins, each Plugin execution generates an Execution Log entry, and Context Memory is associated with both Users and Tasks, providing historical context for current operations.')

